I think the first thing a corporate would need to do is clear up all the 
  IP issues. Nethack has directly lifted IP from Tolkien, Pratchett, 
Moorcock and countless other authors whose estates probably haven't 
agreed to have their work used in that way. Whilst they might overlook 
it as 'good' publicity for now, as soon as someone actively starts 
trying to use Nethack as a advertising medium, then it's all got to go, 
or risk having your name dragged through the mud, and potentially the 
courts.

Which leads me on to... Inviting contemporary sci-fi and fantasy authors 
to donate new, original weapon names and encyclopedia entries. This 
could benefit both their book-sales, and the game. But it would only 
work if the 'nethack' element were used in a larger marketing campaign, 
which raised awareness of both the game and the authors. It could be a 
good excuse to send press releases to the gaming, rpg, and sci-fi 
communities about the authors, and get them exposure where they wouldn't 
normally.

I could definately imagine a few people starting to play nethack just 
because Gaiman, Rowling or Pullman had written a few descriptions, and 
perhaps being 'turned-on' to the other writers.


